# `const Walks = B => class extends B { walk(n) { .. } }`, `class Animal extends Eats(Walks(Base)) {}`: the parent of a class in such an arrow is a value. The names above a layout were gathered along the written bases
# alone and ended there: Animal, the one class of which objects are made, was not known to bear `walk`, the class in the arrow was the only holder of { at, food, walk }, and the test of its shape failed at every object.
# The held reading has seen which class arrives as the parent. The names are gathered past it, and of several holders of all the names one that is only a step of such a chain gives way to the class below it.
# A guess: the shape is tested at the run. The program makes objects of a class in the arrow itself too: those of Eats(Walks(Base)) are asked beside the Animals. All answer the same.
# f1 = f . L0 = the class in Walks, L1 = the class in Eats, L2 = Base, L3 = Animal
# -- source: an-object-of-a-class-below-mixins-is-expected-by-the-names-above-it.cjs
